-- title:
--   Residues of j-c along a level-one prolongation pair
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $q$, a valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, a field $k$ of characteristic $q$ and a ring homomorphism $\mathrm{red} : A \to k$. Let `data` be a `ModularPolynomialData q`, that is a monic $\Phi \in \mathbb{Z}[X][Y]$ of degree $\psi(q)$ annihilating the pair of $q$-expansions, let `hKr` assert the Kronecker congruence that the reduction of $\Phi$ modulo $q$ equals $(C(X)^q - X)(C(X) - X^q)$, and let `hα`, `hβ` assert that the ring homomorphisms underlying `heckeAlphaBar` and `heckeBetaBar` at level $1$ and prime $q$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ are integral. Let $P$ be a `PlaceSpecialization` for these data, $R$ a `LevelOneProlongationPair` for $P$ — in particular a pair of regular prolongations $R_1, R_2$ of $A$ to the field $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}\cdot(\text{modular functions of level } 1\cdot q)$, namely `modularFunctionFieldBar (1 * q)`, with residue maps into the level-one function field `modularFunctionFieldFullC (ResidueField A) 1` and a fixed homomorphism $\iota$ of the latter into `modularFunctionFieldC k 1`, the $k$-subfield of $k((\mathfrak{q}))$ generated by `jqModC k` — and let $c \in A$. The assertion is that the element $j - c$, where $j$ is the coefficientwise image in $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}((\mathfrak{q}))$ of the $\mathfrak{q}$-expansion `jq` and $c$ is viewed as a constant, lies in the integers of both $R_1$ and $R_2$, and that its residues are, in `modularFunctionFieldC k 1`, $$R.\mathrm{residue}_1(j-c) = \tilde{\jmath} - \mathrm{red}(c), \qquad R.\mathrm{residue}_2(j-c) = \tilde{\jmath}^{\,q} - \mathrm{red}(c),$$ with $\tilde{\jmath} =$ `jqModC k`.
--
--   This identifies the reductions of the level-one modular function $j - c$ along the two prolongations of a level-one prolongation pair, the second being the Fricke transport of the first, so that the two residues differ by the $q$-power Frobenius; it is the function-field shadow of the two components of the special fibre of $X_0(q)$ at $q$. It is used in the construction of good representatives of admissible divisor classes, in the two lemmas on $\mathrm{smul}$-translates of point divisors according to whether the relevant residue vanishes.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_PlaceSpecialization_LevelOneProlongationPair_exists_mem_integers_residue_jFun_sub_algebraMap.lean

